00:00:03.651 --> 00:00:05.270
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.140
And by the way, it's always
so exciting in business

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:09.650
when you can keep your promises.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.990
That's what Nan, my partner,

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.440
and the love of my life,
other than my daughters,

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.710
she would always say, keep your promises.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:20.250
If people allow you to use their money,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.370
and take a chance on you in
business, keep your promises.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:26.760
And you're crushing it
for Quibi right now,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.480
and it's so great because then, you know,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.470
it's nice when it works for everybody,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.610
and you get to keep your
job, and they're thrilled.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.160
It's like, then it's good business

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.203
as well as a wonderful
creative opportunity.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:41.240
- Yeah.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.540
Obviously, just like you were saying,

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.970
sometimes you have to
create your own roles.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:50.930
I think whatever you're
doing, whether it's designing,

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.810
or acting, you want to be in a TV show,

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:53.690
or you want to read a book,

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.500
it's like, actually a lot of that stuff

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.470
comes from the person
saying to themselves,

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.510
I wish there was a story about this,

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.080
or, aw, I wish, even with
me with House of Harlow,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.910
I used to be like, I wish I
had like, a kimono, you know?

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.100
You just think about
things that you would want

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:12.990
that don't exist, and you're like,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.050
well, I guess then I will
go and create it myself,

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.070
or write it myself, you know?

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.763
And I think to have those moments,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.440
they're few and far between,

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.450
so when you have that creative spark,

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.840
it's important to just run
after it, take advantage of it.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.700
- I think that is excellent advice,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.170
because you're an amazing brander,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.400
and then this show has
proven to be successful

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.300
for you and Quibi, and
everybody's loving it.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.900
And I think that's a good thing,

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.070
to sort of strike while the iron is hot,

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.260
grab that inspiration when you have it.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.620
You always think in a dream

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.730
you're gonna remember it in the morning-

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:52.563
- [Nicole] Then you don't.

00:01:52.563 --> 00:01:53.396
- And sometimes you don't.

00:01:53.396 --> 00:01:55.050
So I'm equating it to that metaphor.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:59.600
Go after it and catch those
butterflies in your net.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.980
- I remember you saying something to me

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.380
